<h2>Analytic Number Theory — the Riemann Zeta Function, Prime Number Theorem & L-functions</h2>
<p>Analytic number theory studies the distribution of prime numbers using complex analysis. The central object is the <strong>Riemann zeta function</strong> ζ(s) = Σ_{n=1}^∞ n^{-s} for Re s > 1, which admits an analytic continuation to ℂ \ {1} via a functional equation. The <strong>Riemann hypothesis</strong> — that all non-trivial zeros of ζ(s) lie on the line Re s = 1/2 — remains one of the greatest open problems in mathematics. The <strong>Prime Number Theorem</strong> π(x) ∼ x/log x, proved independently by Hadamard and de la Vallée-Poussin in 1896, describes the asymptotic distribution of primes. <strong>Dirichlet L-functions</strong> L(s, χ) generalize ζ(s) to arithmetic progressions, leading to Dirichlet's theorem on primes in arithmetic progressions. The <strong>circle method</strong> (Hardy-Littlewood, Vinogradov) tackles additive problems like Goldbach's conjecture and Waring's problem. <strong>Sieve methods</strong> (Selberg, Bombieri) provide powerful tools for detecting primes and almost-primes. The <strong>Bombieri-Vinogradov theorem</strong> gives the average distribution of primes in arithmetic progressions. The <strong>Green-Tao theorem</strong> (2004) shows the primes contain arbitrarily long arithmetic progressions, using Szemerédi's theorem and a transference principle.</p>
<div class="quote-box"><div class="quote">"The prime numbers are the most mysterious objects studied by mathematicians."</div><div class="attribution">— G. H. Hardy</div></div>
<div class="section-label part1">Part 1</div>
<h2>📏 The Riemann Zeta Function</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Zeta</div><div class="story-title">Definition, Euler Product, Analytic Continuation, and the Riemann Hypothesis</div><div class="story-text">The <strong>Riemann zeta function</strong> is defined for Re s > 1 by ζ(s) = Σ_{n=1}^∞ n^{-s}. It admits the <strong>Euler product</strong>: ζ(s) = Π_p (1 − p^{-s})^{-1}, connecting it to primes. The function extends meromorphically to ℂ \ {1} via the <strong>functional equation</strong>: ζ(s) = 2^s π^{s−1} sin(πs/2) Γ(1−s) ζ(1−s). This gives <strong>trivial zeros</strong> at s = −2, −4, −6, ... (from the sine factor). The <strong>Riemann hypothesis</strong>: all non-trivial zeros ρ satisfy Re ρ = 1/2. The <strong>von Mangoldt function</strong> Λ(n) = log p if n = p^k for some prime p and integer k ≥ 1, and 0 otherwise. The <strong>Chebyshev function</strong> ψ(x) = Σ_{n ≤ x} Λ(n) counts primes weighted by log p. The <strong>explicit formula</strong>: ψ(x) = x − Σ_ρ x^ρ/ρ − log 2π − ½ log(1 − x^{-2}), where the sum is over non-trivial zeros. The <strong>Prime Number Theorem</strong>: π(x) ∼ x/log x, equivalent to ψ(x) ∼ x. The connection between ζ(s) and π(x) comes through the identity: log ζ(s) = s ∫_2^∞ π(x) x^{-s-1} dx for Re s > 1, allowing contour integration to extract asymptotic information.</div><div class="story-moral">The zeta function encodes the distribution of primes through its Euler product; the Riemann hypothesis would give the best possible error term in the Prime Number Theorem.</div></div>
<div class="section-label part2">Part 2</div>
<h2>🌍 Dirichlet Characters and L-functions</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">L-functions</div><div class="story-title">Dirichlet Characters, L-functions, and Primes in Arithmetic Progressions</div><div class="story-text">A <strong>Dirichlet character</strong> χ modulo q is a group homomorphism (ℤ/qℤ)^× → ℂ^×, extended to ℤ by setting χ(a) = 0 if gcd(a, q) > 1. The <strong>principal character</strong> χ₀ mod q satisfies χ₀(a) = 1 if gcd(a, q) = 1 and 0 otherwise. The <strong>Dirichlet L-function</strong> is L(s, χ) = Σ_{n=1}^∞ χ(n) n^{-s} for Re s > 1. It has an <strong>Euler product</strong>: L(s, χ) = Π_p (1 − χ(p) p^{-s})^{-1}. It admits analytic continuation to ℂ and a <strong>functional equation</strong> relating L(s, χ) and L(1−s, χ̄), where χ̄ is the complex conjugate character. <strong>Dirichlet's theorem</strong>: if gcd(a, q) = 1, there are infinitely many primes p ≡ a (mod q). The proof uses the non-vanishing of L(1, χ) for non-principal χ — if L(1, χ) = 0 for some non-principal χ, then the Euler product would contradict the growth of the zeta function. The <strong>class number formula</strong> for a number field K gives the residue of the Dedekind zeta function ζ_K(s) at s = 1 in terms of the class number, regulator, and other invariants. The <strong>Siegel-Walfisz theorem</strong>: for any fixed A > 0, π(x; q, a) = Li(x)/φ(q) + O(x (log x)^{-A}) uniformly for q ≤ (log x)^A, providing a strong form of the prime number theorem for arithmetic progressions for small moduli.</div><div class="story-moral">Dirichlet L-functions generalize the zeta function to arithmetic progressions; their non-vanishing at s = 1 is the key to proving infinitely many primes in arithmetic progressions.</div></div>
<div class="section-label part3">Part 3</div>
<h2>🧮 Sieve Methods</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Sieves</div><div class="story-title">The Sieve of Eratosthenes, Brun Sieve, Selberg Sieve, Large Sieve, and Bombieri-Vinogradov</div><div class="story-text">The <strong>sieve of Eratosthenes</strong> counts primes by inclusion-exclusion over prime divisors. The <strong>Brun sieve</strong> (1919) shows that the number of twin primes ≤ x is O(x/(log x)²), proving that the sum of reciprocals of twin primes converges. The <strong>Selberg sieve</strong> (upper bound sieve) uses quadratic form optimization to choose weights λ_d satisfying Σ_{d|n} λ_d ≥ (Σ_{d|n} μ(d))², giving near-optimal upper bounds on sifted sets. The <strong>large sieve</strong> (Linnik, Bombieri, Gallagher) is a Fourier-analytic method that bounds Σ_q Σ_{a mod q} |Σ_n a_n e(an/q)|², leading to powerful results on the distribution of sequences in arithmetic progressions. The <strong>Bombieri-Vinogradov theorem</strong> (1965): Σ_{q ≤ Q} max_{(a, q)=1} |π(x; q, a) − Li(x)/φ(q)| = O(x^{1/2} Q (log x)^4) for Q ≤ x^{1/2} / (log x)^C. This is the best current approximation to the <strong>Elliott-Halberstam conjecture</strong>, which would allow Q up to x^{1−ε}. An application: the <strong>Friedlander-Iwaniec theorem</strong> (1997) proves infinitely many primes of the form a² + b⁴ using the asymptotic sieve and the Bombieri-Vinogradov theorem. The <strong>twin prime conjecture</strong> states there are infinitely many primes p such that p + 2 is also prime — the Brun sieve gives an upper bound but cannot prove infinitude.</div><div class="story-moral">Sieve methods provide flexible tools for detecting primes and almost-primes; the Bombieri-Vinogradov theorem is a crucial result on average distribution of primes.</div></div>
<div class="section-label part4">Part 4</div>
<h2>🔥 The Circle Method and Recent Breakthroughs</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Circle Method</div><div class="story-title">Hardy-Littlewood Circle Method, Vinogradov, Goldbach, Green-Tao and Bounded Gaps</div><div class="story-text">The <strong>Hardy-Littlewood circle method</strong> expresses the number of representations of an integer N as a sum of k primes as an integral of an exponential sum over the unit circle. The unit circle is split into <strong>major arcs</strong> (neighborhoods of rational points a/q with small q) and <strong>minor arcs</strong> (the complement). Major arcs contribute the main term via singular series; minor arcs are bounded using exponential sum estimates. <strong>Vinogradov's theorem</strong> (1937): every sufficiently large odd integer is a sum of three primes (the ternary Goldbach conjecture). The <strong>Schnirelmann density</strong> of a set A ⊂ ℕ is σ(A) = inf_n A(n)/n; Mann's theorem states that σ(A ⊕ B) ≥ min(1, σ(A) + σ(B)). The <strong>Goldbach conjecture</strong> (binary): every even number > 2 is a sum of two primes — still open. <strong>Chen's theorem</strong> (1973): every sufficiently large even number is a sum of a prime and a number with at most two prime factors (P + P₂). The <strong>Green-Tao theorem</strong> (2004): the primes contain arbitrarily long arithmetic progressions. The proof uses <strong>Szemerédi's theorem</strong> on subsets of ℤ of positive upper density together with a <strong>transference principle</strong> (Gowers norms) to show that the primes — though of zero density — are sufficiently pseudorandom. The <strong>Maynard-Tao theorem</strong> (2019): liminf (p_{n+1} − p_n) ≤ 600, proving bounded gaps between primes. <strong>Zhang's theorem</strong> (2013): liminf (p_{n+1} − p_n) < 70 million, later reduced by the <strong>Polymath8 project</strong> to 246. These results use refinements of the Selberg sieve (GPY method) with the Bombieri-Vinogradov theorem and its generalizations.</div><div class="story-moral">The circle method transforms additive problems into exponential sum estimates; modern breakthroughs combine sieves, pseudorandomness, and ergodic theory to crack the deepest mysteries of primes.</div></div>
